BEGIN_HYPERDESC
<h1>Reentry breakup &mdash; Atlantis</h1>
<p>An entry nobody walks away from: 100 km, <b>12 km/s</b>, 5.4&deg; below the
horizon, belly-first at 40&deg; angle of attack. Superorbital and far too steep
&mdash; a return from beyond the Moon flown straight in instead of skipped.</p>

<p><b>Nothing happens for about thirty-five seconds.</b> Heating climbs as the
air thickens; at roughly 67 km it passes the airframe's limit and the ship
starts tearing pieces off its own mesh. Time-warp 10x through the quiet part if
you would rather not wait for it.</p>

<p>There is no attitude control running, and that is the point &mdash; it enters
belly-first and the air takes the attitude away from it as dynamic pressure
builds, so by the time it comes apart it is flying nose-on. Heating peaks near
<b>250% of the limit</b> around 53 km. It sheds roughly two hundred pieces, then
skips back out and coasts away as a hulk.</p>

<p>Every failure writes a line to <tt>Orbiter.log</tt> naming the heating, the
dynamic pressure, how many pieces came off and how much mass went with them.
The pieces are connected components of Atlantis's own mesh &mdash; they leave a
real hole in the collider as well as in the picture, and nothing in the vessel
folder is touched.</p>

<p>Needs <b>CollisionDetection</b> on the Launchpad's Modules tab and <b>"Damage
and failure simulation"</b> on the Parameters tab, under Vessel. Both are
reported in the log at session start. For the sparks at each tear, <b>Particle
streams</b> has to be on in Visual effects.</p>
END_HYPERDESC

